WEBVTT

00:00.316 --> 00:02.306
So you've got maybe some
Excel under your belt.

00:02.524 --> 00:02.959
Great.

00:03.461 --> 00:05.642
That is exactly who this course is for.

00:05.964 --> 00:10.073
Data engineering is building data
products that can stand behind you

00:10.074 --> 00:11.864
when somebody asks you a hard question.

00:12.355 --> 00:14.550
Nobody is born knowing how to do that.

00:14.842 --> 00:16.184
You learn it by building.

00:16.600 --> 00:18.577
Launch is the full road.

00:18.841 --> 00:24.553
Four phases: foundations, core, platform,
and production, and then a little bit

00:24.554 --> 00:26.086
advanced thrown in there at the end.

00:26.490 --> 00:31.452
About fifty weeks, self-paced
on the labs with one fixed

00:31.455 --> 00:34.050
point, a live review every week.

00:34.320 --> 00:38.076
Plan on eight to twelve hours
a week, and that's real, but

00:38.190 --> 00:39.851
it fits alongside your life.

00:40.503 --> 00:42.137
We don't use clean tutorial data.

00:42.327 --> 00:46.690
From week one, you're gonna be working
with real public feeds, the kind where

00:46.690 --> 00:51.908
the header count disagrees with the rows
and the categories get renamed halfway

00:51.908 --> 00:54.254
through the year because that's the job.

00:54.530 --> 00:56.832
And every week, you don't just build.

00:57.086 --> 01:01.273
You get on the hot seat, and you're
defending what you built out loud.

01:01.744 --> 01:06.164
By the end, you've got a governed
medallion project that produces golden

01:06.166 --> 01:08.244
records, plus a recorded walkthrough.

01:08.595 --> 01:13.056
The kind of project that makes a
hiring manager lean in and go, "Wait,

01:13.443 --> 01:14.714
we're having this problem right now."

01:15.620 --> 01:19.245
Start with phase one and build
something that you can defend
